    "bhashyaExtract": "यथा धूलीपटले धूमभ्रमादपि यथार्थवह्न्यनुमितिर्जायते, तद्वत् व्यावहारिकसत्त्ववता मिथ्याभूतेनापि दृश्यत्वादिलिङ्गेन परमार्थब्रह्मैक्यसिद्धौ न काऽपि व्याहतिः । अज्ञानोपहितसाक्षित्वेऽपि व्यावहारिकताऽविरुद्धैव ।",
    "englishTranslation": "Establishing Valid Probative Efficacy Without Absolute Reality: The Dvaitin contends that reality-proving arguments outweigh falsity-proving ones because if the logical steps (thesis, probans, example) of the Advaita inference are themselves false, the inference undermines itself (sva-vyāhati), since only what is ultimately real can prove anything. Madhusūdana replies: Proving an Advaita conclusion requires only empirical reality (vyāvahārika-sattva)—defined simply as unsublatability by anything short of liberating Brahman-knowledge—NOT absolute triple-time reality! Even when empirical signs (like letters, logical terms, or mistaking a dust-cloud for smoke) are empirically real yet ultimately sublatable, they operate seamlessly according to worldly norms (loka-maryādā). Because empirical reality does not violate empirical logic, proving the falsity of all non-Brahman things (including the syllogism itself upon dawn of final realization) involves zero self-contradiction.",
